  <Groups>
    <Group elementId="CPLX-4583" textLabel="CPLX-4583" type="Complex">
      <Xref identifier="CPLX-4583" dataSource="plantcyc" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="295" width="140" height="100" textColor="666666" fontName="Arial" fontSize="10" borderColor="9900CC" borderStyle="Dashed" borderWidth="2.0" fillColor="F5E6FF" shapeType="Rectangle" zOrder="-1" />
      <Comment>Plants have two types of glutamine synthetase isoenzymes: one located in the cytosol (GS1) and the other in the chloroplast (GS2). The Arabidopsis GS1 is encoded by a small subfamily of five genes, whereas GS2 is encoded by a single gene. GS1 is the major form of glutamine synthetase detected in the root. In leaves, both GS1 and GS2 are abundant. Four genes encoding the cytosolic glutamine synthetase, GLN1;1, GLN1;2, GLN1;3 and GLN1;4 are expressed in the root but in different cell types. GLN1;1 and GLN1;4 have high substrate affinities. GLN1;2 and GLN1;3 have low substrate affinities. GLN1;1 is up-regulated under nitrogen limitation and down-regulated by ammonium excess. GLN1;4 is also up-regulated under nitrogen starvation but in less abundance than GLN1;1. On the other hand, GLN1;2 is up-regulated by ammonium. GLN1;3 is inhibited by a high concentration of glutamate.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="CPLX-4583" />
